Below is the annual "official" /dev/null/nethack announcement.

There are to be a _lot_ of changes this year, largely having to do with
re-writing the whole thing largely from scratch after multiple unrelated disk
failures.  The Trophy structure is staying roughly the same as last year,
though with the addition of a slot for each year to add a new Challenge Trophy.
 No, we won't tell you what it is; that's why it's a Challenge.

The scoreboard will not be functional tomorrow, but we should have all the
Trophies on it by Friday.  Unfortunately, the .nethackrc editor will also not
be ready tomorrow nor will the game recorder file download.  Pretty much
everything else is ready.

On the plus side, the system is _way_ more automated now and much more flexible
since we've now had six years of experience before having to re-write it from
scratch.  ;-)

Feel free to pass it 'round and submit it to any appropriate venues, should you
feel so inclined.

See y'all at midnight.

-robin

#####
The NetHack season is upon us once again.

Tonight (Halloween) at midnight Pacific U.S. Time, /dev/null's Seventh Annual
NetHack Tournament (<a
href="http://nethack.devnull.net">http://nethack.devnull.net</a>) opens. As
with past years, the Tournament is open to anyone who'd like to play. We're
also open to anyone who'd like to volunteer to run a game server since, though
we have servers distributed around North America so far, play can be slow
across the transoceanic links.

/dev/null is a loose association of networking geeks, unincorporated and
noncommercial.  We just do this for giggles; we make no money from this other
than what folks feel like donating.

Due to this fall's disk crashes and all the re-writing they've made necessary ,
the scoreboard will once again not be completely functional at the start of the
Tournament and will probably be changed several times daily for
the first week or so.  ;-)

We're not going to try to describe the Trophy structure in these announcements
anymore; it gets more complicated every year and this section had reached two
screens of text.  A full description is available on the web site at <a
href="http://nethack.devnull.net/tournament/trophies.shtml">http://nethack.devnu
ll.net/tournament/trophies.shtml</a>.

To participate in the Tournament, visit our web site <a
href="http://nethack.devnull.net">http://nethack.devnull.net</a> during the
month and sign up for an account.

Server Requirements:

This year's Tournament will begin with servers in California and Oklahoma but
with additional servers hopefully coming online in the first few days.  Since
we always appreciate additional volunteer servers to help speed the game play
up for folks not near an existing host, here's roughly what you need to
volunteer a game server (Remember, before volunteering, that anyone with admin
level access to a game server box is not allowed to compete; so don't volunteer
to host if you want to be a contender):

	a multi-user 3.4.3 NetHack installation (thus, probably, a Unix of some sort;
the /dev/null server is an OpenBSD system)

	an installed copy of our game server kit (<a
href="http://nethack.devnull.net/software">http://nethack.devnull.net/software</
a>)

	sufficient speed/disk/memory to support 5000 player accounts and 26 concurrent
players (ours used to be a P133 with 32M of memory and 5G of disk, but the
motherboard died; that small a box would still work, though we would recommend
at least 20G of disk)

	a dedicated Internet connection (ours is on a T1; cable modems and DSL will
work fine, but it's dificult to do unless you have static IP numbers)

	since NetHack needs real user accounts, we'd recommend that it be a dedicated
system (the players will need to be entered in /etc/passwd to play on the box)
